WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.183
Two hundred people, made-up data

2
00:00:05.183 --> 00:00:06.982
and two numbers for each

3
00:00:06.982 --> 00:00:09.610
how many minutes they exercise a day

4
00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:11.340
and their resting pulse.

5
00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:13.570
Each dot is one person

6
00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.450
we draw forty of them.

7
00:00:15.532 --> 00:00:17.902
People who exercise more tend

8
00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:19.782
to have a lower pulse

9
00:00:19.782 --> 00:00:22.970
so the dots form a long, tilted cloud.

10
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.829
Can one direction describe this cloud?

11
00:00:26.829 --> 00:00:30.983
Last time, the SVD found the main directions

12
00:00:31.076 --> 00:00:32.091
of a table.

13
00:00:32.091 --> 00:00:35.600
Pause and guess where it points here.

14
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.859
Try the obvious thing

15
00:00:40.859 --> 00:00:43.224
take the SVD of the raw table.

16
00:00:43.301 --> 00:00:45.514
The first direction comes out

17
00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.498
as zero point three nine

18
00:00:47.498 --> 00:00:49.100
zero point nine two.

19
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:51.130
Draw it from the origin.

20
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.950
It points straight at the

21
00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.420
middle of the cloud

22
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.240
along the average person.

23
00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.250
It only says that people have about thirty

24
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.630
minutes and a pulse near seventy.

25
00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:02.610
We knew that.

26
00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:06.997
The fix is step one of PCA: centre the data.

27
00:01:06.997 --> 00:01:10.717
Subtract the average person from everyone

28
00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:13.053
so the middle of the cloud

29
00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.870
moves to the origin.

30
00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.610
Now every dot is measured from the centre.

31
00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:22.162
Zoom in, using the same scale on both axes

32
00:01:22.162 --> 00:01:24.180
so the angles stay true.

33
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.805
Step two: the SVD of the centred table.

34
00:01:28.805 --> 00:01:32.954
The first right singular vector, v one

35
00:01:32.954 --> 00:01:35.443
is zero point nine one

36
00:01:35.443 --> 00:01:38.244
minus zero point four one.

37
00:01:38.244 --> 00:01:42.600
It runs along the long axis of the cloud.

38
00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.330
v two is at a right angle

39
00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.435
across the short axis.

40
00:01:47.527 --> 00:01:50.547
The singular values are about one

41
00:01:50.639 --> 00:01:53.110
ninety one and forty three.

42
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:54.418
Step three

43
00:01:54.506 --> 00:01:58.339
the share of the spread along v one is sigma

44
00:01:58.426 --> 00:02:01.912
one squared over the sum of the squares.

45
00:02:01.912 --> 00:02:03.741
Ninety five percent.

46
00:02:03.741 --> 00:02:04.961
This ellipse

47
00:02:04.961 --> 00:02:07.749
two typical distances each way

48
00:02:07.749 --> 00:02:11.060
shows how long and thin the cloud is.

49
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.245
Each person also gets a score

50
00:02:14.245 --> 00:02:16.676
their position along v one.

51
00:02:16.676 --> 00:02:18.673
Take the first person

52
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.278
forty minutes of exercise and

53
00:02:21.365 --> 00:02:23.275
a pulse of sixty four.

54
00:02:23.275 --> 00:02:25.880
Drop them onto the long axis.

55
00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.236
They land eleven point seven

56
00:02:28.308 --> 00:02:29.884
units from the centre.

57
00:02:29.884 --> 00:02:32.606
One number per person instead of two

58
00:02:32.606 --> 00:02:35.830
and only five percent of the spread is lost.

59
00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:38.664
Compare the simplest way to go

60
00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:40.542
from two numbers to one

61
00:02:40.617 --> 00:02:42.871
keep only the exercise minutes

62
00:02:42.946 --> 00:02:44.374
and drop the pulse.

63
00:02:44.449 --> 00:02:46.552
That squashes every dot onto

64
00:02:46.628 --> 00:02:48.130
the horizontal axis.

65
00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.520
That keeps eighty percent of the spread.

66
00:02:51.596 --> 00:02:53.572
The direction found by PCA

67
00:02:53.648 --> 00:02:56.840
mixes both columns, and keeps ninety five.

68
00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.455
Many books do PCA another way

69
00:03:00.455 --> 00:03:03.491
with the covariance matrix C.

70
00:03:03.491 --> 00:03:07.032
C is the centred table transposed

71
00:03:07.032 --> 00:03:11.180
times the table, divided by n minus one.

72
00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.433
Its eigenvectors are the

73
00:03:13.512 --> 00:03:15.177
same v one and v two.

74
00:03:15.257 --> 00:03:18.269
Its eigenvalues are one hundred eighty

75
00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:20.489
three and nine point three

76
00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:23.264
exactly the squared sigmas divided

77
00:03:23.344 --> 00:03:24.850
by one ninety nine.

78
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.467
And the long axis is the line

79
00:03:27.546 --> 00:03:29.109
closest to the dots

80
00:03:29.109 --> 00:03:31.846
in squared perpendicular distance.

81
00:03:31.924 --> 00:03:34.973
That is the best rank one approximation

82
00:03:35.051 --> 00:03:37.240
of the table, from part one.

83
00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.016
A check for working with AI.

84
00:03:40.094 --> 00:03:42.996
Suppose an assistant reports that the

85
00:03:43.074 --> 00:03:46.133
first component explains sigma one over

86
00:03:46.211 --> 00:03:48.172
sigma one plus sigma two

87
00:03:48.172 --> 00:03:49.740
eighty two percent.

88
00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:52.535
But spread is a sum of squares

89
00:03:52.535 --> 00:03:54.665
so use the squared sigmas.

90
00:03:54.665 --> 00:03:57.820
The right share is ninety five percent

91
00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:01.370
the same as the covariance eigenvalues give.

92
00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.353
Low rank also makes large AI

93
00:04:04.439 --> 00:04:06.498
models cheaper to adapt.

94
00:04:06.584 --> 00:04:09.415
One layer of a network multiplies

95
00:04:09.501 --> 00:04:11.303
by a weight matrix W

96
00:04:11.303 --> 00:04:14.993
four thousand and ninety six on each side

97
00:04:14.993 --> 00:04:17.910
almost seventeen million numbers.

98
00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:21.387
The method called LoRA keeps W fixed

99
00:04:21.473 --> 00:04:24.687
and learns a small change, B times A.

100
00:04:24.687 --> 00:04:27.726
B is tall with only eight columns

101
00:04:27.726 --> 00:04:30.853
and A is wide with only eight rows.

102
00:04:30.853 --> 00:04:34.500
Drawn to scale, they are just thin lines.

103
00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:36.759
Together they hold sixty

104
00:04:36.838 --> 00:04:38.588
five thousand numbers

105
00:04:38.588 --> 00:04:41.053
less than half a percent of W.

106
00:04:41.053 --> 00:04:44.076
And B times A has rank at most eight

107
00:04:44.155 --> 00:04:46.382
exactly the kind of matrix a

108
00:04:46.461 --> 00:04:48.370
few SVD pieces describe.

109
00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:53.245
So, PCA in three steps: centre the table

110
00:04:53.245 --> 00:04:57.958
take the SVD, read the long axis from v one

111
00:04:58.063 --> 00:05:00.891
and measure the shares with

112
00:05:00.996 --> 00:05:03.510
squared singular values.

113
00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:05.127
Next lecture

114
00:05:05.127 --> 00:05:09.025
the SVD solves any system A x equals b

115
00:05:09.025 --> 00:05:12.728
even when there is no exact solution

116
00:05:12.728 --> 00:05:14.580
the pseudoinverse.
